✅ What Is JDK?
JDK (Java Development Kit) is the software development kit used to build Java applications.
It includes tools for compiling, debugging, running, and monitoring Java programs.

📌 Without JDK → you cannot compile/develop Java programs (traditionally).
   With only JRE → you can run already-compiled .class / JAR files.

Nested picture (interview fav):
  JDK
   └── JRE
        └── JVM
  Plus: compiler + other dev tools (javac, jar, javadoc, …)


🧠 How JDK Works (Conceptual Flow)

Java Source Code (.java)
        ↓
javac (Compiler)          ← JDK tool
        ↓
Bytecode (.class)
        ↓
java (Launcher)           ← starts JVM
        ↓
JVM
        ↓
Execution (interpret + JIT)


📦 JDK = JRE + Development Tools

🔹 JRE (Java Runtime Environment)
- Lets you RUN Java apps
- Contains: JVM + core class libraries + supporting files
- JDK install already includes a runtime (so you get JRE capabilities with JDK)

Libraries note (version-aware):
  Pre–Java 9 → rt.jar (and friends) held the core classes
  Java 9+    → modular runtime (jmods / modules), rt.jar removed


🛠 JDK Internal Tools & Utilities

| Tool        | Purpose                                      |
| ----------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `javac`     | Compiler (.java → .class)                    |
| `java`      | Launches JVM to run .class / JAR             |
| `javadoc`   | HTML docs from Javadoc comments              |
| `jar`       | Create / list / extract JAR files            |
| `jdb`       | Command-line debugger                        |
| `jshell`    | REPL (Java 9+)                               |
| `javap`     | Disassemble .class (bytecode view)           |
| `jdeps`     | Analyze class/module dependencies            |
| `jlink`     | Build custom minimal runtime image (Java 9+) |
| `jconsole`  | GUI JVM monitoring                           |
| `jvisualvm` | Profiling / memory analysis                  |

(Detail of these → see "Core Java Command-Line Tools" notes)


🧰 JDK Folder Structure (on disk)

| Folder/File | Description                                       |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `bin/`      | Executables: javac, java, jar, javadoc, …         |
| `lib/`      | Libraries / tool support                          |
| `include/`  | C/C++ headers for JNI                             |
| `jmods/`    | Module files for jlink (Java 9+)                  |
| `conf/`     | Config (security, logging, …)                     |
| `src.zip`   | JDK class library source (read/debug in IDE)      |


🔁 Lifecycle of a Java Program (How JDK Is Used)

1) Write:  Hello.java
2) Compile: javac Hello.java  → Hello.class
3) Run:     java Hello        → JVM loads & executes bytecode


💬 Common Interview Questions

🔹 Q1: Difference JDK vs JRE vs JVM?
  JDK → Develop + Run (tools + runtime)
  JRE → Only Run (JVM + libraries)
  JVM → Executes bytecode (engine inside JRE)

🔹 Q2: What happens on compile & run?
  .java --javac--> .class (bytecode)
  java loads class into JVM
  JVM interprets and/or JIT-compiles to native code → runs

🔹 Q3: Can we run Java without JDK?
  Yes — runtime is enough if you already have .class / JAR
  No — you cannot compile without JDK (javac)

🔹 Q4: Is JRE still downloaded separately?
  Conceptually yes (run-only). Modern Oracle/OpenJDK often ship JDK as the main install;
  runtime is included inside JDK. Idea of JRE still asked in interviews.


🔍 Important Notes
- Java 9+ → JPMS modules
- 6-month release cycle; LTS examples: 11, 17, 21
- OpenJDK = open-source reference implementation
- Multiple JDKs OK → switch via JAVA_HOME / PATH


📌 Summary (Interview)
JDK = full toolkit for Java development = JRE + compiler + tools.
javac turns source into bytecode; java starts the JVM to run it.
Need JDK to build; runtime alone is enough only to run.
